IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate

-- Electoral Act 1907 (Western Australia) -- the State elector: who is entitled to be enrolled and to vote (s 17), enrolment
-- at 16 and 17 (s 17AA), the people barred (s 18), the arrangements with the Commonwealth for one enrolment (ss 31, 31A, 35A)
-- as far as they reach the elector, the offences of not being enrolled and of not notifying a new address, with their bars on
-- proceedings (s 45), and compulsory voting (Part IV Division 7: ss 155AB, 156): the duty, the valid and sufficient reason,
-- the penalty notice and its modified penalty, the infringement notice, removal from the register, and the fines.
--
-- SEAT: the State elector -- the person who may enrol, must be enrolled and must vote, and who is sent the penalty notice.
-- Every decision is phrased as that person would ask it ("Am I entitled to be enrolled", "Must I vote", "What is the most the
-- modified penalty can be"). The Electoral Commissioner appears only as what may be done to the elector, and by when.
--
-- Edition: compilation from legislation.wa.gov.au (mrdoc 47953, retrieved 7 September 2026), as deposited at
-- ../../registers/source-bundle/mrdoc_47953.txt. The deposit names no compilation number or date; its compilation table ends
-- with the Electoral Amendment (Names of Registered Political Parties) Act 2024 (No. 46 of 2024, in force 16 November 2024).
-- No regulation is used: the Electoral Regulations 1996 (WA) are not deposited. The "prescribed period" for sending a penalty
-- notice (s 156(4)) and the prescribed forms are theirs, and are not encoded; the modified penalty actually set, up to the
-- Act's cap, is the Commissioner's, and is an INPUT where needed.
--
-- NOT ENCODED: administration (Part II), districts and the whole of State electorate beyond s 156(1) (Parts IIA, IIB); the
-- register's mechanics (ss 19, 34, 35, 38-44A, 46 onwards: claims, objections, review); s 17(4) (members and spouses); ss 17A
-- and 17B (eligible overseas and itinerant electors, who keep State enrolment through their Commonwealth annotation); silent
-- electors (s 51B) beyond s 17AA(2)(c); s 51A; how votes are cast (provisional, postal, early); nominations, polling, scrutiny;
-- s 156AA (evidentiary certificates) and s 156(2a), (3) (the list as evidence); s 156(17) (who may prosecute); everything after
-- s 156. The text is the CURRENT compilation: Chloe's 2021 election (c024) was held under an earlier text that was not deposited
-- (s 17AA, s 45 in its present form and the whole of State electorate came later: see NOTES.md).
--
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Written straight from the deposited text with the writing-l4-rules skill, from the seat of the
-- individual to whom the law is applied, with the design defaults ruled on 2026-10-18. No pipeline, no coverage table, no
-- independent test pass, no human gate. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `Electoral Act 1907 (WA) -- the State elector`

-- Where my name stands on the register of electors. "Enrolled" (s 4, s 40A): my details, including my district, are entered on
-- the register of electors. "District" (s 4): an electoral district for the election of a member of the Assembly.
DECLARE `My enrolment` IS ONE OF
    `not enrolled`
    `enrolled for the district I live in`
    `enrolled for a district I have moved out of`

-- The facts the elector can state. Borrowed terms (temporary visa and unlawful noncitizen: Migration Act 1958; the ground in
-- Commonwealth Electoral Act 1918 s 93(8)(a); a sentence under the Young Offenders Act 1994 or the Sentencing Act 1995) are
-- asked as facts. A "lack of capacity notice" (s 4, s 206A(3)) is asked as a fact; s 206A is not encoded.
DECLARE `My circumstances` HAS
    `my date of birth`                                                          IS A DATE
    `I am an Australian citizen`                                                IS A BOOLEAN
    `I am a British subject who was an elector within 3 months before 26 January 1984` IS A BOOLEAN
    `I hold a temporary visa or am an unlawful noncitizen`                      IS A BOOLEAN
    `I am the subject of a lack of capacity notice`                             IS A BOOLEAN
    `I am off the Commonwealth roll for this State on the ground in Commonwealth section 93(8)(a)` IS A BOOLEAN
    `I was convicted of treason and have not been pardoned`                     IS A BOOLEAN
    `I am serving or yet to serve detention or imprisonment of 1 year or longer` IS A BOOLEAN
    `I am under indefinite imprisonment, a Criminal Code order or supervision`  IS A BOOLEAN
    `the day I began living in my district`                                     IS A DATE
    `my enrolment`                                                              IS A `My enrolment`

§§ `Age and residence`

-- s 17(1)(b) "has attained 18 years of age"; s 17AA(1)(a) "has attained 16 years of age". INFERENCE (the Act does not say): a
-- person attains an age at the start of the anniversary of birth; a birthday of 29 February is taken as 28 February in other
-- years (daydate's `add years` clamps). The same reading as the Commonwealth Electoral Act baseline.
@export Have I reached a given age on a given day?
GIVEN `me`      IS A `My circumstances` @desc Your circumstances; only your date of birth is read
      `the age` IS A NUMBER             @desc The age in whole years
      `the day` IS A DATE               @desc The day to test, ISO 8601, e.g. 2025-03-08
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Have I reached the age` IF
    Day `the day` AT LEAST Day (`add years` (`me`'s `my date of birth`) `the age`)

-- s 17(1)(c): "has lived in the same district for at least 1 month immediately before the enrolment". "1 month" is read as one
-- calendar month from the day residence began (INFERENCE), as in the Commonwealth baseline's s 99.
@export Have I lived in my district for at least 1 month on a given day (s 17(1)(c))?
GIVEN `me`      IS A `My circumstances` @desc Your circumstances; the day you began living in your district is read
      `the day` IS A DATE               @desc The day to test, ISO 8601
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Have I lived in my district for a month` IF
    Day `the day` AT LEAST Day (`add months` (`me`'s `the day I began living in my district`) 1)

§§ `18 -- people not entitled to vote and be enrolled`

-- s 18(1): "A person is not entitled to vote in an election if the person -- (a) is the subject of a lack of capacity notice;
-- or (aa) is not on the Commonwealth roll for this State under the ground specified in the Commonwealth Electoral Act 1918
-- section 93(8)(a); or (b) has been convicted of treason and has not been granted a pardon for the offence; or (c) is serving
-- or is yet to serve a sentence of detention (imposed under the Young Offenders Act 1994), or imprisonment, of 1 year or longer;
-- or (ca)-(cd)" [indefinite imprisonment, Criminal Code orders, supervised persons] "or (d) is the holder of a temporary visa
-- ... or is an unlawful noncitizen". s 18(2): "A person mentioned in subsection (1)(a), (aa), (b) or (d) is not entitled to be
-- enrolled as an elector." So paragraphs (c)-(cd) take the vote but not the enrolment.
@export Am I barred from being enrolled by section 18(2)?
GIVEN `me` IS A `My circumstances` @desc Your circumstances
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Am I barred from enrolment` IF
        `me`'s `I am the subject of a lack of capacity notice`
    OR  `me`'s `I am off the Commonwealth roll for this State on the ground in Commonwealth section 93(8)(a)`
    OR  `me`'s `I was convicted of treason and have not been pardoned`
    OR  `me`'s `I hold a temporary visa or am an unlawful noncitizen`

@export Am I barred from voting by section 18(1)?
GIVEN `me` IS A `My circumstances` @desc Your circumstances
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Am I barred from voting` IF
        `Am I barred from enrolment` `me`
    OR  `me`'s `I am serving or yet to serve detention or imprisonment of 1 year or longer`
    OR  `me`'s `I am under indefinite imprisonment, a Criminal Code order or supervision`

§§ `17 -- who is entitled to be enrolled and vote`

-- s 17(1): "Subject to the provisions of this Act, any person -- (a) who is -- (i) an Australian citizen; or (ii)" a non-citizen
-- who would be a British subject and "was at some time within 3 months, immediately preceding 26 January 1984, an elector of the
-- Assembly or an elector, under a Commonwealth Act, of the Commonwealth Parliament; and (b) who has attained 18 years of age; and
-- (c) who has lived in the same district for at least 1 month immediately before the enrolment, is entitled -- (d) to be
-- enrolled". "Subject to the provisions of this Act" brings in s 18(2).
@export Apart from my age and residence, am I qualified to be enrolled (s 17(1)(a), s 18(2))?
GIVEN `me` IS A `My circumstances` @desc Your circumstances
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Am I qualified apart from age and residence` IF
        (    `me`'s `I am an Australian citizen`
         OR  `me`'s `I am a British subject who was an elector within 3 months before 26 January 1984`)
    AND (NOT `Am I barred from enrolment` `me`)

@export Am I entitled to be enrolled for my district on a given day (s 17(1))?
GIVEN `me`      IS A `My circumstances` @desc Your circumstances
      `the day` IS A DATE               @desc The day to test, ISO 8601
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Am I entitled to be enrolled` IF
        `Am I qualified apart from age and residence` `me`
    AND `Have I reached the age` `me` 18 `the day`
    AND `Have I lived in my district for a month` `me` `the day`

-- The day the s 17(1) entitlement begins: the later of the 18th birthday and a month in the district. Every other qualification
-- is taken to have held throughout (the same simplification as the Commonwealth baseline).
@export On what day did I become entitled to be enrolled for my district (s 17(1))?
GIVEN `me` IS A `My circumstances` @desc Your circumstances
GIVETH A DATE
DECIDE `On what day did I become entitled to be enrolled` IS
    IF Day `my 18th birthday` AT LEAST Day `a month in my district`
    THEN `my 18th birthday`
    ELSE `a month in my district`
    WHERE
        `my 18th birthday`      MEANS `add years` (`me`'s `my date of birth`) 18
        `a month in my district` MEANS `add months` (`me`'s `the day I began living in my district`) 1

-- s 4: "elector means a person who is enrolled". s 17AA(2): until a person enrolled at 16 or 17 attains 18, the person "(a) is
-- taken not to be an elector; and (b) is not entitled to vote in an election".
@export Am I an elector on polling day (s 4, s 17AA(2)(a))?
GIVEN `me`              IS A `My circumstances` @desc Your circumstances
      `the polling day` IS A DATE               @desc The polling day, ISO 8601
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Am I an elector` IF
        (NOT `me`'s `my enrolment` EQUALS `not enrolled`)
    AND `Have I reached the age` `me` 18 `the polling day`

-- s 17(1)(e): "when so enrolled and while continuing to live in that district, to vote in -- (i) any Council election; and (ii)
-- any election in the district", with s 18(1). "election means a Council election or an election in a district" (s 4). Read
-- alone, s 17(1)(e) gives no vote to an elector who has moved out of the district: whether another provision does (for example
-- on transfer of enrolment) is NOT ENCODED, and the answer here is the s 17(1)(e) answer only.
@export Am I entitled to vote at a State election with this polling day (s 17(1)(e), s 17AA(2), s 18(1))?
GIVEN `me`              IS A `My circumstances` @desc Your circumstances
      `the polling day` IS A DATE               @desc The polling day, ISO 8601
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Am I entitled to vote` IF
        `me`'s `my enrolment` EQUALS `enrolled for the district I live in`
    AND `Have I reached the age` `me` 18 `the polling day`
    AND `Am I qualified apart from age and residence` `me`
    AND (NOT `Am I barred from voting` `me`)

-- s 17(2), (3): a person who "fulfils the requirements of subsection (1)(a) and (b)", has lived in a district "for a period of at
-- least 1 month ending immediately before the day ... on which the person intends to vote", and on that day "is not enrolled, or
-- is not enrolled for the relevant district" and "presents at a place to vote", is "entitled to be enrolled for the relevant
-- district" and, "if the person makes a claim for enrolment, or a claim for enrolment or transfer of enrolment under the
-- Commonwealth Electoral Act 1918 to be enrolled on a Commonwealth roll for this State, entitled to vote in the election on the
-- relevant day as a provisional voter under section 97G". (Subject to ss 145(7) and 172(1)(c), on re-run elections: not
-- encoded.) That s 18(1) still bars the vote is an INFERENCE from "Subject to the provisions of this Act" and s 18(1)'s words.
-- The month is read as ending on the day before polling day, so the residence must have begun at least a month before that.
@export Can I enrol on polling day and vote as a provisional voter (s 17(2), (3))?
GIVEN `me`              IS A `My circumstances` @desc Your circumstances
      `the polling day` IS A DATE               @desc The day you would vote, ISO 8601
      `I present at a place to vote and make a State or Commonwealth claim` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether on that day you present at a place to vote and make a claim for enrolment under this Act or under the Commonwealth Electoral Act 1918
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Can I enrol on polling day and vote provisionally` IF
        `Am I qualified apart from age and residence` `me`
    AND `Have I reached the age` `me` 18 `the polling day`
    AND `Have I lived in my district for a month` `me` (`the polling day` MINUS 1)
    AND (NOT `me`'s `my enrolment` EQUALS `enrolled for the district I live in`)
    AND `I present at a place to vote and make a State or Commonwealth claim`
    AND (NOT `Am I barred from voting` `me`)

§§ `17AA -- enrolment at 16 and 17`

-- s 17AA(1): "A person is entitled to be enrolled for a district if -- (a) the person has attained 16 years of age but has not
-- attained 18 years of age ...; and (b) the person would be entitled to be enrolled for the district if the person had attained
-- 18 years of age." Section 17AA was inserted by the Electoral Amendment (Finance and Other Matters) Act 2023 (No. 30 of 2023)
-- s 16, whose Part 2 came into operation on 1 July 2024 (compilation table). Before that day the deposited text gives no such
-- entitlement; what the earlier text said was not deposited. s 17AA(3) (a person who does not know their age) is not encoded.
-- It is a right, not a duty: s 45(1) excepts a person entitled "under section 17AA(1)".
GIVETH A DATE
`the day section 17AA came into operation` MEANS Date 1 7 2024

@export May I enrol for my district at 16 or 17 (s 17AA(1))?
GIVEN `me`      IS A `My circumstances` @desc Your circumstances
      `the day` IS A DATE               @desc The day you would claim, ISO 8601
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `May I enrol at 16 or 17` IF
        Day `the day` AT LEAST Day `the day section 17AA came into operation`
    AND `Have I reached the age` `me` 16 `the day`
    AND (NOT `Have I reached the age` `me` 18 `the day`)
    AND `Am I qualified apart from age and residence` `me`
    AND `Have I lived in my district for a month` `me` `the day`

-- s 17AA(2)(c): until 18 the person enrolled at 16 or 17 "is taken to be a silent elector".
@export If I enrolled at 16 or 17, am I treated as a silent elector on a given day (s 17AA(2)(c))?
GIVEN `me`      IS A `My circumstances` @desc Your circumstances
      `the day` IS A DATE               @desc The day to test, ISO 8601
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Am I taken to be a silent elector because of my age` IF
        (NOT `me`'s `my enrolment` EQUALS `not enrolled`)
    AND (NOT `Have I reached the age` `me` 18 `the day`)

§§ `45 -- compulsory enrolment`

-- The Act lays no express duty to claim on the elector (s 38(2)(a) lets regulations do so; none is deposited). The duty is
-- carried by the offence in s 45(1): "A person who is entitled to be enrolled for a district, otherwise than under section
-- 17AA(1), and who is not enrolled for the district at the end of the period of 21 days beginning on the day they become
-- entitled to be enrolled, or on any subsequent day while they continue to be entitled to be enrolled, commits an offence.
-- Penalty for this subsection: a fine of $50."
@export Must I be enrolled for my district (s 45(1))?
GIVEN `me`      IS A `My circumstances` @desc Your circumstances
      `the day` IS A DATE               @desc Today, ISO 8601
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Must I enrol` IF
        `Am I entitled to be enrolled` `me` `the day`
    AND (NOT `me`'s `my enrolment` EQUALS `enrolled for the district I live in`)

-- "the period of 21 days beginning on the day they become entitled": the day of entitlement is day 1, so the period ends at the
-- end of that day plus 20. This is ONE DAY EARLIER than the Commonwealth s 101(4), whose 21 days run "from the date upon which
-- the person became so entitled" (that baseline: entitlement plus 21). NOTHING if I am not qualified apart from age and
-- residence, so that no day of entitlement comes.
@export What is the last day by which I must be enrolled to avoid the s 45(1) offence?
GIVEN `me` IS A `My circumstances` @desc Your circumstances
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
DECIDE `What is the last day by which I must be enrolled` IS
    IF `Am I qualified apart from age and residence` `me`
    THEN JUST (`On what day did I become entitled to be enrolled` `me` PLUS 20)
    ELSE NOTHING

-- s 45(4): "A person does not contravene subsection (1) if they prove that their nonenrolment is not because of their failure to
-- send or deliver to the Electoral Commissioner a claim in the prescribed form". (5): nor if not enrolled "because the person has
-- a physical or mental impairment".
@export Have I committed the s 45(1) offence of not being enrolled?
GIVEN `me`      IS A `My circumstances` @desc Your circumstances
      `the day` IS A DATE               @desc The day to test, ISO 8601
      `I can prove it is not because I failed to send a claim` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you can prove that you are not enrolled for some reason other than your own failure to send a claim
      `it is because of a physical or mental impairment`       IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are not enrolled because you have a physical or mental impairment
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Have I committed the offence of not being enrolled` IF
        `Must I enrol` `me` `the day`
    AND Day `the day` GREATER THAN Day (`On what day did I become entitled to be enrolled` `me` PLUS 20)
    AND (NOT `I can prove it is not because I failed to send a claim`)
    AND (NOT `it is because of a physical or mental impairment`)

-- s 45(7): "If a person sends a claim for enrolment to the Electoral Commissioner or lodges a claim for enrolment under the
-- Commonwealth Electoral Act 1918, proceedings must not be instituted against the person for any offence against subsection (1)
-- before the claim was sent or the person was enrolled." A claim lodged with the AEC is enough.
@export Can I still be prosecuted for not being enrolled (s 45(1), (4), (5), (7))?
GIVEN `me`      IS A `My circumstances` @desc Your circumstances
      `the day` IS A DATE               @desc The day of the offence, ISO 8601
      `I can prove it is not because I failed to send a claim` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you can prove that you are not enrolled for some reason other than your own failure to send a claim
      `it is because of a physical or mental impairment`       IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are not enrolled because of a physical or mental impairment
      `I have since sent a State claim or lodged a Commonwealth claim, or been enrolled` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether since then you have sent a claim to the Electoral Commissioner, lodged a claim under the Commonwealth Electoral Act 1918, or been enrolled
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Can I be prosecuted for not being enrolled` IF
        `Have I committed the offence of not being enrolled` `me` `the day` `I can prove it is not because I failed to send a claim` `it is because of a physical or mental impairment`
    AND (NOT `I have since sent a State claim or lodged a Commonwealth claim, or been enrolled`)

-- s 45(2): "A person (including a silent elector) who is enrolled for a district, and who changes their primary residential
-- address from 1 address in the district to another address in the district, must, within 21 days after the change, give notice
-- in writing of the new address to the Electoral Commissioner. Penalty for this subsection: a fine of $50." INFERENCE on the
-- count: the last day is the day of the change plus 21. The Commonwealth s 101(5) runs 21 days from a month at the new address;
-- the State runs them from the change itself.
@export By when must I give written notice of a new address in the same district (s 45(2))?
GIVEN `the day I moved` IS A DATE @desc The day you changed your primary residential address within your district, ISO 8601
GIVETH A DATE
DECIDE `By when must I give notice of my new address` IS `the day I moved` PLUS 21

-- s 45(5): no contravention if the failure is "because the person has a physical or mental impairment". s 45(8): no proceedings
-- for an offence committed before a written notice was sent, or a claim lodged under the Commonwealth Act.
@export Can I be prosecuted for not giving notice of my new address (s 45(2), (5), (8))?
GIVEN `the day I moved` IS A DATE @desc The day you changed address within your district, ISO 8601
      `the day`         IS A DATE @desc The day to test, ISO 8601
      `it is because of a physical or mental impairment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the failure is because of a physical or mental impairment
      `I have since given notice or lodged a Commonwealth claim` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have since given written notice of the new address, or lodged a claim for enrolment under the Commonwealth Electoral Act 1918
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Can I be prosecuted for not notifying my new address` IF
        Day `the day` GREATER THAN Day (`By when must I give notice of my new address` `the day I moved`)
    AND (NOT `it is because of a physical or mental impairment`)
    AND (NOT `I have since given notice or lodged a Commonwealth claim`)

§§ `31, 31A, 35A -- the Commonwealth roll and the State register`

-- The Act keeps a State register (s 19) separate from the Commonwealth roll, and joins them only by arrangement. s 31(1): "The
-- Governor may arrange with the Governor General of the Commonwealth for the Australian Electoral Officer to perform" the
-- receipt of claims under this Act and "the transmission to the Electoral Commissioner of the information necessary for changes
-- to be made to the register"; (2)(c): "a claim shall be in a form provided by the Electoral Commissioner and the Australian
-- Electoral Officer for putting into effect a single enrolment procedure for the purpose of Commonwealth and State elections";
-- (3): the Governor may by Gazette order "declare that a single enrolment procedure is in operation". s 31A(1): an arrangement
-- for the AEO to notify the Commissioner of each person whose Commonwealth enrolment is updated or transferred under Commonwealth
-- s 103A, or who is entered under Commonwealth s 103B. s 35A(2): "If the Electoral Commissioner considers, based on received
-- information about a person, that the person is entitled to be enrolled, the Electoral Commissioner may enrol the person."
-- Whether an order or arrangement is in operation is not stated in the deposit: INPUTS. That a claim on the single form is a
-- claim under this Act is an INFERENCE from s 31(1)(a) and (2)(c).
DECLARE `How I came onto the Commonwealth roll` IS ONE OF
    `by my own claim`
    `directly by the Australian Electoral Commission under Commonwealth section 103A or 103B`

DECLARE `What reaches the State register` IS ONE OF
    `my claim is also my State claim, received by the Australian Electoral Officer`
    `the Electoral Commissioner may enrol me on the notified information`
    `nothing reaches it: I must send a State claim`

@export If I am enrolled on the Commonwealth roll, does that reach the State register (ss 31, 31A, 35A)?
GIVEN `how`                                       IS A `How I came onto the Commonwealth roll` @desc How you came onto the Commonwealth roll for an address in Western Australia
      `a single enrolment procedure is in operation` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a Gazette order under s 31(3) declares a single enrolment procedure in operation
      `an information-sharing arrangement is in operation` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether an arrangement under s 31A(1) for notifying direct Commonwealth enrolments is in operation
GIVETH A `What reaches the State register`
DECIDE `Does my Commonwealth enrolment reach the State register` IS
    CONSIDER `how`
    WHEN `by my own claim` THEN
        IF `a single enrolment procedure is in operation`
        THEN `my claim is also my State claim, received by the Australian Electoral Officer`
        ELSE `nothing reaches it: I must send a State claim`
    WHEN `directly by the Australian Electoral Commission under Commonwealth section 103A or 103B` THEN
        IF `an information-sharing arrangement is in operation`
        THEN `the Electoral Commissioner may enrol me on the notified information`
        ELSE `nothing reaches it: I must send a State claim`

§§ `156(1), (1A), (16) -- the duty to vote and the offence`

-- s 156(1): "An elector must vote in any election for the whole of State electorate, and in any election for the district for
-- which the elector is enrolled, unless prevented from doing so by absence from the State, or because the person has a mental or
-- physical impairment on the days on which voting occurs in the election." s 16C(1): the whole of State electorate elects the
-- Council.
DECLARE `What happened at the election` HAS
    `I voted`                                                         IS A BOOLEAN
    `I was outside the State, and that prevented me voting`           IS A BOOLEAN
    `I had a mental or physical impairment on the voting days`        IS A BOOLEAN
    `I honestly believe abstaining from voting is part of my religious duty` IS A BOOLEAN
    `I had another valid and sufficient reason`                       IS A BOOLEAN
    `the Commissioner is satisfied of my absence, ineligibility or reason` IS A BOOLEAN

-- The duty is laid on "an elector" (s 4: a person enrolled), not on a person entitled to vote. INFERENCE (not express), the same
-- as the Commonwealth baseline's: an elector whom s 18(1) bars from voting (for example a prisoner serving a year or more, who
-- stays enrolled) has no duty to vote; s 156(5)(c) lets the Commissioner withhold a notice from one who "was ineligible to vote".
@export Must I vote at a State election with this polling day (s 156(1))?
GIVEN `me`              IS A `My circumstances`             @desc Your circumstances
      `the polling day` IS A DATE                           @desc The polling day, ISO 8601
      `what happened`   IS A `What happened at the election` @desc What happened at the election
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Must I vote` IF
        `Am I an elector` `me` `the polling day`
    AND `Am I entitled to vote` `me` `the polling day`
    AND (NOT `what happened`'s `I was outside the State, and that prevented me voting`)
    AND (NOT `what happened`'s `I had a mental or physical impairment on the voting days`)

-- s 156(1A): "a valid and sufficient reason in relation to a failure to vote includes an honest belief on the part of an elector
-- that abstaining from voting is part of their religious duty". Otherwise undefined: an INPUT.
@export Do I have a valid and sufficient reason for not voting (s 156(1A))?
GIVEN `what happened` IS A `What happened at the election` @desc What happened at the election
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Do I have a valid and sufficient reason` IF
        `what happened`'s `I honestly believe abstaining from voting is part of my religious duty`
    OR  `what happened`'s `I had another valid and sufficient reason`

-- s 156(16): "If an elector fails to vote in an election without a valid and sufficient reason, the elector commits an offence.
-- Penalty for this subsection: a fine of $200." Reading the offence as needing the s 156(1) duty (so that absence from the State
-- or impairment answers it even if not called a reason) is an INFERENCE.
@export Have I committed the offence of failing to vote (s 156(16))?
GIVEN `me`              IS A `My circumstances`             @desc Your circumstances
      `the polling day` IS A DATE                           @desc The polling day, ISO 8601
      `what happened`   IS A `What happened at the election` @desc What happened at the election
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Have I committed the offence of failing to vote` IF
        `Must I vote` `me` `the polling day` `what happened`
    AND (NOT `what happened`'s `I voted`)
    AND (NOT `Do I have a valid and sufficient reason` `what happened`)

§§ `156(2)-(8) -- the penalty notice`

-- s 156(2): a list of "the electors who were on the roll ... and did not vote". (4): "within the prescribed period after the close
-- of each election, the Electoral Commissioner shall send a penalty notice to each elector whose name appears on the list". (5):
-- "The Electoral Commissioner does not have to send a penalty notice to an elector if the Electoral Commissioner is satisfied that
-- the elector -- (a) is dead; or (b) was outside the State on polling day; or (c) was ineligible to vote in the election; or (d)
-- had a valid and sufficient reason for failing to vote." The Commissioner's satisfaction is an input, answered conditionally;
-- paragraph (a) is not a question the elector asks. The prescribed period is in regulations not deposited.
@export Must the Electoral Commissioner send me a penalty notice (s 156(2), (4), (5))?
GIVEN `me`              IS A `My circumstances`             @desc Your circumstances
      `the polling day` IS A DATE                           @desc The polling day, ISO 8601
      `what happened`   IS A `What happened at the election` @desc What happened at the election
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Must I be sent a penalty notice` IF
        `Am I an elector` `me` `the polling day`
    AND (NOT `what happened`'s `I voted`)
    AND (NOT (    `what happened`'s `the Commissioner is satisfied of my absence, ineligibility or reason`
              AND (    `what happened`'s `I was outside the State, and that prevented me voting`
                   OR  (NOT `Am I entitled to vote` `me` `the polling day`)
                   OR  `Do I have a valid and sufficient reason` `what happened`)))

-- s 156(7): "The response date set out in a penalty notice shall not be less than 21 days after the day on which the notice is
-- sent." (13): the same for an infringement notice.
@export Is the response date in my notice lawful (s 156(7), (13))?
GIVEN `the day the notice was sent` IS A DATE @desc The day the notice was sent, ISO 8601
      `the response date`           IS A DATE @desc The response date set out in the notice, ISO 8601
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is the response date lawful` IF
    Day `the response date` AT LEAST Day (`the day the notice was sent` PLUS 21)

-- s 156(8): "The modified penalty set out in a penalty notice must be an amount not exceeding -- (a) if the elector has not
-- previously paid a modified penalty under this section or been convicted of an offence against this section -- $50; or (b)
-- otherwise -- $75." (13): the same caps for an infringement notice. A penalty paid under the Commonwealth Electoral Act 1918
-- s 245, or a fine under s 45 of this Act, is not "under this section".
@export What is the most the modified penalty in my notice can be, in dollars (s 156(8), (13))?
GIVEN `I have paid a modified penalty or been convicted under section 156 before` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have previously paid a modified penalty under s 156 of this Act, or been convicted of an offence against it (a Commonwealth penalty does not count)
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `What is the most the modified penalty can be` IS
    IF `I have paid a modified penalty or been convicted under section 156 before` THEN 75 ELSE 50

§§ `156(6), (11)-(15A) -- responding, the infringement notice, removal`

-- s 156(6)(c): by the response date the elector may "(i) if the elector did vote ... give the Electoral Commissioner particulars
-- of the circumstances of the elector's voting; or (ii) if the elector failed to vote -- give the Electoral Commissioner a valid
-- and sufficient reason for the failure; or (iii) pay to the Electoral Commissioner the penalty set out in the notice (the
-- modified penalty)". (11): if the Commissioner is satisfied on (i) or (ii), or the elector pays, "proceedings are not to be taken
-- against the elector for a contravention of subsection (16)".
DECLARE `My response to the penalty notice` IS ONE OF
    `no response by the response date`
    `particulars of my voting, and the Commissioner is satisfied`
    `particulars of my voting, but the Commissioner is not satisfied`
    `a reason, and the Commissioner is satisfied`
    `a reason, but the Commissioner is not satisfied`
    `I paid the modified penalty by the response date`

@export Does my response to the penalty notice stop a prosecution (s 156(11))?
GIVEN `my response` IS A `My response to the penalty notice` @desc How you responded to the penalty notice by its response date
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Does my response stop a prosecution` IF
    CONSIDER `my response`
    WHEN `particulars of my voting, and the Commissioner is satisfied` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `a reason, and the Commissioner is satisfied`                 THEN TRUE
    WHEN `I paid the modified penalty by the response date`            THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

-- s 156(12), (13A): if the elector does not respond by the response date, or responds under (6)(c)(i) or (ii) and the
-- Commissioner is not satisfied, "the Electoral Commissioner may send to the elector an infringement notice", offering a modified
-- penalty instead of prosecution. "May": the Commissioner's choice.
@export May I be sent an infringement notice (s 156(12), (13A))?
GIVEN `my response` IS A `My response to the penalty notice` @desc How you responded to the penalty notice
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `May I be sent an infringement notice` IF
    NOT (`Does my response stop a prosecution` `my response`)

-- s 156(14): paying the infringement notice's modified penalty by its response date means "proceedings are not to be taken
-- against the elector for a contravention of subsection (16)(a)". Section 156(16) has no paragraph (a) in this compilation; read
-- as (16).
@export Can I still be prosecuted for failing to vote (s 156(11), (14), (16))?
GIVEN `I committed the offence`            IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you failed to vote without a valid and sufficient reason (s 156(16))
      `my response`                         IS A `My response to the penalty notice` @desc How you responded to the penalty notice
      `I paid the infringement notice in time` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were sent an infringement notice and paid its modified penalty by its response date
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Can I be prosecuted for failing to vote` IF
        `I committed the offence`
    AND (NOT `Does my response stop a prosecution` `my response`)
    AND (NOT `I paid the infringement notice in time`)

-- s 156(14a): "If an elector is unable, by reason of absence from their place of living or physical incapacity, to respond to a
-- penalty notice or to an infringement notice on or before the response date, any other elector who has a personal knowledge of
-- the facts may, subject to the regulations, respond to the notice within that time".
@export If I cannot respond myself, may another elector respond for me (s 156(14a))?
GIVEN `I am away from my place of living or physically incapacitated` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether absence from your place of living or physical incapacity stops you responding by the response date
      `they are an elector with personal knowledge of the facts`      IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the other person is an elector who personally knows the facts
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `May another elector respond for me` IF
        `I am away from my place of living or physically incapacitated`
    AND `they are an elector with personal knowledge of the facts`

-- s 156(15): at the conclusion of an election the Commissioner must find out whether a person who "failed or neglected to respond
-- to a penalty notice or an infringement notice is living at the address shown in the roll" and, "if the Electoral Commissioner is
-- satisfied that the person is no longer living at that address, cause the name of that person to be removed from the register".
-- (15A): the person "must make a new claim for enrolment before their name is reinstated".
@export Must my name be removed from the register for not responding (s 156(15))?
GIVEN `I failed to respond to a penalty or infringement notice` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you failed or neglected to respond to a penalty notice or an infringement notice
      `the Commissioner is satisfied I no longer live at my roll address` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Commissioner is satisfied that you no longer live at the address shown in the roll
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Must my name be removed from the register` IF
        `I failed to respond to a penalty or infringement notice`
    AND `the Commissioner is satisfied I no longer live at my roll address`

§§ `45, 156(16), (16A) -- the fines, as a table`

-- The elector's offences in these sections, each with its fine as written in the Act (the State Act states dollars, not penalty
-- units). s 45(1), (2): "a fine of $50"; s 156(16): "a fine of $200"; s 156(16A): a statement in response to a notice "that the
-- elector knows to be false or misleading in a material particular": "a fine of $1 000".
DECLARE `My offence` IS ONE OF
    `not enrolled after 21 days, s 45(1)`
    `not notifying a new address in the district, s 45(2)`
    `failing to vote, s 156(16)`
    `a false or misleading response to a notice, s 156(16A)`

@export What is the most I can be fined for this offence, in dollars (ss 45, 156(16), (16A))?
GIVEN `the offence` IS A `My offence` @desc The offence
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `What is the most I can be fined` IS
    CONSIDER `the offence`
    WHEN `not enrolled after 21 days, s 45(1)`                    THEN 50
    WHEN `not notifying a new address in the district, s 45(2)`   THEN 50
    WHEN `failing to vote, s 156(16)`                             THEN 200
    WHEN `a false or misleading response to a notice, s 156(16A)` THEN 1000
